  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}\u20611].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}\u20611-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true.",
